    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Add an explicit 'Requires: soapy-sdr' - to the module subpackage only.

Requested by dimstar reviewing the soapy-plutosdr submission (sr#1374694,
since superseded to sr#1374739):
&quot;Shouldn't that require soapy-sdr? The module on its own seems quite useless&quot;.
Applied across the SoapySDR plugin set rather than to that one package.

To be precise about what this does and does not do: libSoapySDR is what dlopens
a plugin, and that is already covered by the automatic soname dependency on
libSoapySDR.so.0.8-3 - it was never missing. What a module package did not pull
in is the soapy-sdr package proper, which ships SoapySDRUtil, the tool used to
enumerate and probe devices. That is the gap this closes.

Note on precedent: no SoapySDR plugin currently released in Factory carries this
dependency (checked soapy-rtlsdr, soapy-hackrf, soapy-airspy, soapy-osmo,
soapy-uhd), so this is a deliberate tightening rather than an existing
convention.

soapy-remote is not a plain plugin, so the dependency is deliberately NOT
applied uniformly across its subpackages:

- soapysdr0.8-3-module-remote ships only the dlopened client plugin
  libremoteSupport.so, under the ABI-versioned SoapySDR module directory. This
  is the plugin dimstar's argument is about, and it gets the Requires.

- soapy-remote-server ships the SoapySDRServer daemon, its systemd unit, a
  sysctl.d drop-in and the man page. That is a standalone application, not a
  plugin: it links libSoapySDR directly (and so already carries the automatic
  libSoapySDR.so.0.8-3 soname dependency) and uses nothing from the soapy-sdr
  package - the string &quot;SoapySDRUtil&quot; does not occur anywhere in the 0.5.2
  source tree. Adding the Requires there would be redundant, so it is not.

The code shared between the two halves (SoapySDRRemoteCommon) is a static
library that is never installed, so the client plugin is the only shared object
this package ships and there is no soname question here.

Also in this request, packaging cleanup with no change to the installed file
set: %cmake_build instead of an open-coded make line, the %{_sysctldir} macro
instead of a hand-built path, 'mkdir -p' for the sbindir so the build does not
break once /usr/sbin is merged and the directory already exists, and the
SoapySDR floor upstream actually checks for - CMakeLists.txt has
find_package(SoapySDR &quot;0.4.0&quot; NO_MODULE), so pkgconfig(SoapySDR) &gt;= 0.4.0.

Left alone deliberately: Conflicts: soapysdr0.7-module-remote is not stale -
soapysdr0.7-module-remote is still published in openSUSE:Backports:SLE-15-SP1
(4 binaries), SP2 (4) and SP3 (5).

No version bump. 0.5.2 (2020-07-20) is still upstream's newest tag, and Debian,
Gentoo, Arch and FreeBSD all ship it. master is 30 commits ahead with a
&quot;Release 0.6.0 (pending)&quot; changelog, but upstream has released none of it and it
relocates the systemd and sysctl install paths (and renames the drop-in), so
moving to a pinned snapshot is a separate decision rather than something to fold
into a dependency fix.

Also fixed here: device discovery over mDNS/DNS-SD was silently disabled.
common/CMakeLists.txt calls find_package(Avahi) and on failure issues only a
CMake WARNING, not a FATAL_ERROR, then compiles SoapyMDNSEndpointNone.cpp - a
no-op stub - in place of SoapyMDNSEndpointAvahi.cpp. So the package built green
while one of its two discovery mechanisms was compiled out. Adding
BuildRequires: pkgconfig(avahi-client) (Factory's libavahi-devel, which carries
avahi-client.pc plus the client/common libraries and headers that upstream's
FindAvahi.cmake probes) fixes it. Confirmed from the build log rather than
inferred from a green build: cmake now reports &quot;Found Avahi&quot;, prints
AVAHI_LIBRARIES=/usr/lib64/libavahi-common.so;/usr/lib64/libavahi-client.so, the
warning is gone, and SoapyMDNSEndpointAvahi.cpp is what lands in the static
library. Both subpackages consequently gain libavahi-client.so.3 and
libavahi-common.so.3; the installed file set is unchanged.

Enabling Avahi exposed a second, run-time half of the same bug, fixed here by
SoapySDRServer-order-after-avahi.patch. Upstream's mDNS registration is
one-shot: avahi_entry_group_new() fails if the daemon is not yet on the bus,
and registerService() returns at that point - before the avahi_simple_poll_loop
task is armed - so the client never leaves CONNECTING and nothing ever
re-registers. AVAHI_CLIENT_NO_FAIL buys survival, not recovery. Meanwhile
nothing ordered the two units: avahi-daemon.service carries no After= at all
and SoapySDRServer.service only ordered itself after network-online.target, so
both were merely WantedBy=multi-user.target. Losing that race would have moved
the exact defect this request fixes from build time to run time. The patch adds
Wants=/After=avahi-daemon.service; avahi-daemon is Type=dbus, so After=
guarantees the bus name is owned, and Wants= on an uninstalled unit is a no-op,
which keeps it consistent with the weak Recommends below. The patch is
upstreamable - it is a defect in upstream's own unit file - and has not been
sent yet.

Both subpackages also get Recommends: avahi rather than a hard Requires. The
daemon is what actually advertises and browses, so it is wanted - but upstream
opens the client with AVAHI_CLIENT_NO_FAIL and guards the null client, so its
absence degrades gracefully, and SSDP discovery is unaffected either way.
Debian uses a hard Depends: avahi-daemon on both; a weak dependency seemed the
better fit for openSUSE, and is easy to change if reviewers prefer otherwise.

Built locally on Tumbleweed/aarch64; rpmlint reports 0 errors, 0 warnings,
0 badness.</comment>
    <requestid>1374759</requestid>
